        <h1 class="page-title">Tenant Rights Guide for US Renters</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">Everything you need to know about renting in the United States</p>

        <p>Renting a home comes with legal protections that many tenants don't fully understand. Whether you're dealing with a security deposit dispute, a maintenance issue, or facing eviction, knowing your rights can make a significant difference in how these situations are resolved.</p>

        <h2>Security Deposits</h2>
        <p>Most states require landlords to return your security deposit within a specific timeframe after you move out, typically ranging from 14 to 45 days depending on the state. Landlords can only deduct money for unpaid rent, damage beyond normal wear and tear, or required cleaning. They must usually provide an itemized list of any deductions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Document the condition of your unit with photos when you move in and out</li>
          <li>Request your deposit back in writing if your landlord doesn't return it on time</li>
          <li>Many states allow you to sue for double or triple the deposit if landlords act in bad faith</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>The Right to a Habitable Home</h2>
        <p>Every tenant has the right to a livable rental unit. This is known as the "implied warranty of habitability." Landlords must maintain working plumbing, heating, electrical systems, and keep the property free from health hazards. If your landlord refuses to make necessary repairs, you may have options including repair-and-deduct remedies, depending on your state's laws.</p>

        <h2>Protection from Illegal Eviction</h2>
        <p>Landlords cannot simply lock you out, remove your belongings, or shut off utilities to force you out — this is called a "self-help eviction" and is illegal in every state. Landlords must go through the proper court process, which typically involves a formal notice followed by a court filing if you don't move out voluntarily.</p>

        <h2>Protection from Discrimination</h2>
        <p>The federal Fair Housing Act prohibits landlords from discriminating based on race, color, religion, sex, national origin, familial status, or disability. Many states add additional protected categories such as sexual orientation or source of income.</p>

        <h2>What to Do If Your Rights Are Violated</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Keep all communication with your landlord in writing</li>
          <li>Research your specific state's tenant laws, as rules vary significantly</li>
          <li>Contact a local tenant rights organization for free guidance</li>
          <li>Consider small claims court for disputes involving money, like deposits</li>
          <li>Consult a licensed attorney for serious issues like eviction</li>
        </ul>

